WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.320
We are looking at an economy in which
the rich get richer and the poor get poorer.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.460
Middle class is declining. That trend doesn't seem
to be slowing down.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:17.100
America's middle class is getting hollowed out and opportunity for too many of our young people

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.640
is shrinking. A lot of people think the middle class is dead, dying, hollowed out.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:27.900
And that's a view that's held now increasingly by not just the left where it was common 10, 20 years ago,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.920
but by conservatives, Republicans, economists across the spectrum.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.600
Russ Roberts is an economist, author and research fellow at Stanford University's Hoover institution.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.540
I think it's just a misreading of the data, at least an incomplete reading of the data,

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:47.620
it's ignoring the pieces of the story that that I think tell a much fuller story of opportunity and progress.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.800
Robert says that the findings of several of the leading studies are determined in part

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.840
by the index that the authors use to adjust for rising prices over time.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.300
Turns out the measure inflation you choose makes a big difference. In one study,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.860
they found that one measure of progress for the middle class went down 7% over a 30, 40 year period.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.980
Wow, that's a terrible thing because the economy as a whole group grew quite a bit,

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.280
but if researchers had used a more comprehensive measure of inflation

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.600
published by the Bureau of Economic Analysis, they would have found a 17% improvement.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.300
But Roberts points out that even that 17% growth doesn't accurately portray the extent

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.700
to which the middle-class standard of living has improved since the 1970s.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.640
What's not captured by either index is the dramatic improvement in the quality of consumer goods.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.380
In the 1970s for example,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.440
TV's were heavy boxes with 12 inch screens, rabbit ears,  attenae, and a fuzzy picture.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.100
Today for the same dollar amount as in 1977 you can buy a computer with about

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.840
one and a half million times the hard drive space and about 2 million times the RAM.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:56.420
The quality of groceries, healthcare, and cars, are incomparable to what was sold in the 1970s.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.920
And the list goes on and on.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.160
Yet because technological advances are difficult to quantify, they're generally ignored.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.260
Lets take the following thought experiment.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.200
I'm gonna give you your income today and
I'm gonna let you shop at 1973 prices,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.440
but you can only buy goods available and services available in 1973, so they're going to be really cheap.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.520
You're going to have a ton of money
to spend. Is that a good deal?

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.840
So most of us would say, I don't want to live in that 1970s world.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.800
Not only am I not  five times better off, I might be actually worse off.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.060
Which tells you that the ways we correct for inflation are really flawed.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.900
A 2017 study by Thomas Piketty, Emmanuel Saez, and Gabriel Zucman contained a widely cited chart

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.760
that was written up in the New York Times, Vox, and other news outlets.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:47.920
It allegedly showed that the real incomes of the bottom 50% stagnated between 1979 and 2014.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.460
Roberts points out that by taking a snapshot of the income levels at one point in the past

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:56.000
and comparing it to a snapshot of income levels in 2014,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.080
studies of this sort make a common methodological error.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.040
What this approach misses is that these are not the same people.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.940
Nothing in the data says that those who made up the middle class in the 70s

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:12.060
weren't doing significantly better in 2014. It's misleading to use the kinds of data found in this study

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.360
to support the claim that all the gains from economic growth go to the rich.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.520
A lot of the people are getting those gains today or getting those high salaries

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.820
or people who are poor or, or in the middle 10, 20, 30 years ago but have entered the top.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.480
And the top is higher today than it used to be.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.220
So it's gonna look like the rich got richer, but it isn't true if they're not the same people.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.300
So how do you correct for that? Well, there's a handful of studies that look at the same people over time.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.320
So instead of saying, let's see what's happening, people in 1975 and let's see what's happening, people in say

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:50.280
2015. Let's instead take people in 1975 and let's follow them through time and let's see how they did.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.580
And did the rich get all the gains or, was it the poor?

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.000
You find out that the people at the bottom have the largest percentage gains

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.080
and often the largest absolute gains over time. Now, what are the people who are pessimistic?

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.860
What do they do with that, those, those studies, how do they interpret those studies? They typically ignore them.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.340
This fuller picture shows that the American middle-class hasn't done as poorly as politicians and journalists

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.860
would have us believe, but there's still room for improvement.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.520
And there are many things I don't like about economic policy in the United States.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.980
There's a lot of cronyism we should get rid of. There are a lot of barriers for the poor.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.460
We give them a horrible, the poor, a horrible education through the public school system.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.840
So there's a lot of things to fix. It's not like everything's, you know, great.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.320
But we don't want to say, oh well the whole system's rigged cause it's not.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.000
The average person can make a lot of progress and has, and to ignore that?

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.100
We shouldn't romanticize stuff. It doesn't make us gloriously happier.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.100
But we also don't want to conclude that the system is rigged

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.220
simply because the measurements we have of economic progress are flawed.

